    "contract": [
      "Bounded 513-item ingestion, at most 512 copied native instructions, exact binary/sections/current MethodDef/metadata identity, unique exact method candidate; shared/generic/constructor contexts decline.",
      "Only exact immutable bytes backing is accepted; bytearray, memoryview and other custom backing decline before indexing, avoiding stale unwind snapshots after same-object writes.",
      "NativeUnwindBoundary verifies a common handler-free PE AMD64 unwind root and fresh native bytes. Every supported normal CFG successor must exist; registered interiors, indirect/external flow, missing fallthrough and entry backedges decline.",
      "Closed parameter and known return ABI checks account for receiver/sret and Win64 positional slots. Only non-byref Int32 entry GPRs seed origins.",
      "Actual execution-block identity and native instruction equality; legacy register MOVD GPR32-to-XMM last writer and single-predecessor contiguous suffix. All reaching predecessors must agree through exact MOV32 copies; volatile calls kill provenance under Win64 ABI.",
      "Iterative dependency traversal; suffix steps and distinct native (IP, register) states share the unchanged total 512 budget. Cycles, conflicts, partial/wide/noncopy writers and unsupported contexts decline.",
      "Processor-state save/restore, privileged instructions and ENCLU decline before ordinary GPR/XMM write-list reasoning. The component reads no symbolic register map and mutates no Expr."
    ],
    "limits": [
      "Not a full native semantics verifier, complete EH/CFG decoder, callee-preservation proof, C# binding/lifetime proof or enabled recovery. It validates supplied fresh footprint closure and assumes standard Win64 nonvolatile call preservation.",
      "Exact per-execution entry Expr binding, immutable captures, conversion replacement, hygienic final placement and atomic fallback remain to be implemented in production.",
      "Unwind lookup ownership is currently per plan. Before broad integration, use a verified per-binary lifecycle or lazy eligible-site construction rather than retaining one complete PE index per method."
    ]
  },
